 ## Background
-ExBluRF was introduced by Dongwoo Lee, Sunghyun Cho, Joon-Young Lee, Kyoung Mu Lee, et al. in 2023 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of previous NeRF-based deblurring methods like DeblurNeRF [S1] and BAD-NeRF [S1], which struggled with extreme blur due to high computational costs or inability to model complex 6-DOF trajectories. The method builds on the principles of differentiable volume rendering [44-radiance-field-optimization.md] and voxel-based representations [10852-voxel-based-representation.md]. It was validated on the newly proposed ExBlur dataset, which contains multi-view extremely blurred images with ground truth sharp pairs and trajectories [S1].
+ExBluRF was introduced by Dongwoo Lee, Sunghyun Cho, Joon-Young Lee, Kyoung Mu Lee, et al. in 2023 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of previous NeRF-based deblurring methods like DeblurNeRF [S1] and BAD-NeRF [S1], which struggled with extreme blur due to high computational costs or inability to model complex 6-DOF trajectories. The method builds on the principles of differentiable volume rendering [44-radiance-field-optimization.md] and voxel-based representations [10852-voxel-based-representation.md] [G]. It was validated on the newly proposed ExBlur dataset, which contains multi-view extremely blurred images with ground truth sharp pairs and trajectories [S1].
 
